+++# M14 bugs3 p2-coop-fps: Shape Race co-op, the TV's choppy frames with two players (B-016)
+++
+++## Summary
+++
+++Root cause: a race draws the split screen, two whole boards, and every board rebuilt its still drawing
+++from scratch every frame. Per frame each empty target built its outline twice and then cut its dashes
+++with `paintDashedEdge`, which calls `Path.computeMetrics()` and `extractPath()` for every dash — of the
+++order of twenty paths a target. At a race's start that is two boards of twelve targets: roughly 500
+++paths and 24 `PathMetric` walks a frame, plus a blurred `MaskFilter.blur` slot per target, five laid-out
+++`TextPainter`s, and a game `CustomPaint` that shared a layer with the sky, so the sky was rasterized
+++with every frame too. A solo or team round draws one board, which is why only two players show it.
+++
+++What changed:
+++
+++- `board_cache.dart` (new): `BoardCache` keeps one board's still drawing — `path(slot, key, build)`,
+++  `picture(slot, key, record)`, `dispose()`; exported from the package.
+++- `slot_paint.dart`, `puzzle_painter.dart`: a target's slot, its dashed edge and the mat are recorded
+++  once and drawn again while their key (`_stillKey`) is unchanged; `_targetPath(i)` is built once.
+++- `mark_paint.dart` (new part): the ✓/✗ marks, `_checkPlace` on the cached target paths.
+++- `match_painter.dart`, `match_phone_hud.dart` (new part): each board gets its own cache; the phone
+++  HUD moved out to keep the file under 380 lines.
+++- `view.dart`: the views own the caches (one per board), dispose them, and draw the game in a
+++  `RepaintBoundary` of its own, so the sky is not rasterized with every frame.
+++- `text_paint.dart`: laid-out HUD texts kept, at most 64, keyed by every input.
+++- Tests: `board_cache_test.dart` (5), `repaint_boundary_test.dart` (2 widget tests).
+++
+++Not done: interpolation (the host is authoritative and runs at 60 Hz, so there is no remote frame to
+++interpolate); the pieces, workers and celebration still rebuild each frame; the slot blur is recorded
+++but still rasterized.
+++
+++On a device: watch a solo round and a 1v1 on one TV with Flutter's performance overlay; the 1v1 should
+++now cost about what a solo round did before. If it is still choppy, note the TV model.
+++
+++## What I found (per frame, split screen, two boards)
+++
+++1. Each empty target built its outline `Path` twice, then cut the dashes: `paintDashedEdge` (toy_look)
+++   computes the path's metrics and extracts one path per dash. ~500 paths a frame at a race's start.
+++2. A blurred, clipped slot (`toySunkenSlot`, `MaskFilter.blur`) per empty target, per frame.
+++3. Each locked piece's ✓ ran `_checkPlace`, which built a path for every later empty target: O(n²).
+++4. Five `TextPainter`s laid out per frame: each board's clock/name/count and the strip's clock and two
+++   counts, re-shaping the same short strings.
+++5. The game's `CustomPaint` shared a layer with `ToyStillSky` (a fragment shader) and the screens over
+++   it, so every frame of the game re-rasterized them.
+++6. No `saveLayer` anywhere in `puzzle_game/lib` or `party_host/lib/src/ui` (searched).
+++7. No per-frame cost proportional to history; `SplitLayout.of` is already cached per puzzle/seed/target;
+++   `PuzzleTwins.rotation` is a table lookup. Nothing here needed changing.
+++
+++Interpolation is not applicable on the host: `PuzzlePlayView` runs its own 60 Hz ticker over
+++`PuzzleSession`'s fixed steps, so the dots it draws come from the same simulation, and `FrameView`
+++already lerps received frames on a *viewer* (a phone), which the owner's 60 Hz phone stats show works.
+++
+++## Tests and computed values
+++
+++- `test/board_cache_test.dart`: the same slot and key record once (`recorded == 1`, the same `Picture`);
+++  a changed key records again (`recorded == 2`, a different picture); two slots keep a picture each
+++  (`recorded == 2`); a path is built once (`built == 1`, identical); a key that differs in scale
+++  records again (1 then 2); after `dispose()` the same key records afresh (2). Fails on the old code:
+++  `BoardCache` does not exist.
+++- `test/repaint_boundary_test.dart`: the `RenderCustomPaint` behind `Key('puzzleView')` has a
+++  `RenderRepaintBoundary` as its parent, for `PuzzleView`, for a two-board `MatchView` and for a
+++  one-board `MatchView`. Fails on the old code (the parent was the box's own render object).
+++- Everything else passes unchanged: all 71 test files in `packages/puzzle_game` (in batches, since the
+++  whole suite runs past the 10-minute command limit here), `packages/party_host` (396), `tv_test_app`
+++  (53 passed, 1 skipped as before), and `dart analyze --fatal-infos` over the workspace plus
+++  `dart format` (763 files, none changed). The pixel tests (`view_test`, `portrait_hud_png_test`,
+++  `placed_look_png_test`, `puzzles_png_test`, `worker_*_png_test`) draw through the new cached path and
+++  come out byte-identical, which is the check that recording changed no drawing.
+++
+++## Decisions
+++
+++- Cache *pictures*, not just paths: the CPU cost is the dash cutting, and recording keeps the existing
+++  drawing code exactly as it was — no second dashing rule beside toy_look's.
+++- The cache belongs to the view's `State` and is handed to the painter (the painter is made anew each
+++  frame): it depends on the board's field, scale and look, and two views may draw the same puzzle, so a
+++  static per-puzzle store would draw one view's board with another's.
+++- The cache is optional (`cache` / `caches` empty): painters made directly, as many tests do, draw every
+++  frame as before, so no existing test had to change.
+++- Slots and edges are cached per target index rather than as one picture for the board, because empty
+++  targets' outlines interleave with placed pieces in the picture's order (a door's outline stays over a
+++  wall).
+++- The text memo is library-wide (like `SplitLayout`'s layout cache) and keyed by every input, rather
+++  than plumbed through ten call sites.
+++- `view.dart` and `match_painter.dart` were over or near the 380-line limit, so the marks and the phone
+++  HUD moved to their own part files in the same job.
+++
+++## Suggestions for later (not done)
+++
+++- Cache the pieces, workers and celebration as pictures too; the carried piece and the boing would
+++  have to bypass the cache, so it wants device numbers first.
+++- Drop or cheapen the empty slot's blur on a TV if it still costs: the recorded slot still rasterizes a
+++  blur per target per frame.
+++- Give the HUD a layer of its own (a `CustomPaint` above the board's) if the clock's once-a-second
+++  re-layout ever shows.
+++
+++## Self-check: docs/review-rules.md part A
+++
+++1. **Every host setup.** One shared view: a TV/PC host draws two boards, each with its own cache
+++   (`view_test`, `repaint_boundary_test`); "Play on this phone" and "Show on this device" run the same
+++   code; a joined phone draws one side or the whole match (`portrait_hud_test`, `mirror_test`); an older
+++   build is unaffected, as no snapshot or protocol changed (`snapshot_v2_test`).
+++2. **When kept state starts and ends.** A `BoardCache` starts empty in the view's `initState`, fills as
+++   the still layers are drawn, and every picture is freed in `dispose()`; the text memo is bounded at
+++   64 and disposes what it evicts. Nothing is kept while no view is up. Tested by `board_cache_test`
+++   ("a cache let go records afresh").
+++3. **How a new state combines with others.** Nothing pauses, hides or blocks: a pause simply stops the
+++   frames and the cache holds what was last drawn; the results card draws over the same layer; leaving
+++   disposes the state and its cache. No interaction with host away, player away, a drop or Get ready
+++   (`view_test`'s pause test, `party_host`'s round tests).
+++4. **Stay in the job's folder.** Only `docs/briefs/m14/bugs3/p2-coop-fps-report.md` and
+++   `packages/puzzle_game` (`lib`, `test`) changed.
+++5. **Stop rather than overstep.** No file outside `allow:` was needed. The brief's interpolation item
+++   does not apply to a TV host (above); reported, not acted on.
+++
+++## Questions
+++
+++1. Is the slot/edge blur on a TV worth its cost, or may it be dropped there in a later job?
+++2. Should the piece layer get the same picture cache next, or wait for device numbers?
+++
+++## Commit message
+++
+++Shape Race: keep a board's still layers between frames
+++
+++A race's TV frame rebuilt every board's slots, dashed edges and target
+++paths from scratch, cutting each dash with PathMetrics — about 500 paths a
+++frame for two boards, plus a blurred slot, five text layouts and a game
+++layer that shared the sky's. The view now keeps a BoardCache per board
+++(recorded pictures and target paths, freed on dispose), asks for them by
+++key each frame, remembers laid-out HUD text, and draws the game in its own
+++RepaintBoundary. Drawing is unchanged: every pixel test passes as before.
+++
+++## Status row
+++
+++B-016: the split screen's per-frame cost is cut (board still layers cached, HUD text kept, game in its own layer, tests added); still open: a device measurement on the TV, and a cache for the pieces and workers.
+++
